#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "9ec1a675-6563-5374-85fd-871da49caf30" :question/math-academy-id "e-3727" :question/problem "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5$, and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points,\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nNext, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points\n\n$$\nx = -2, 0, 2\n$$\n\nand evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-2) &= 6 - 3(-2)^{2} =-6 \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 6 - 3(0)^{2} = 6 \\\\\nf^{′}(2) &= 6 - 3(2)^{2} =-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2}$, the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2}$, the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nand one local maximum at\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/id #uuid "3f70f692-38d3-5b7e-9bbb-9fc089679fa0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3729" :question/problem "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) =3x^2\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-1) &= 3(-1)^{2} = 3 > 0 \\\\\nf^{′}(1) &= 3(1)^{2} = 3 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0$. Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a82def66-255f-5658-9109-ab7966855c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3730" :question/problem "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (2x + 3)e^{x} + 2e^{x} = (2x + 5)e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 5)e^{x} &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-5) &= (2(-5) + 5)e^{-5} =-\\frac{5}{e^{5}} < 0 \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= (2(0) + 5)e^{0} = 5 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nthe first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3428605-2071-5dfd-af29-d6ef0c96c0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103007} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d9d7a0-e9aa-5f50-8538-06afaaab65a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103008} {:answer/id #uuid "0d43707b-ee5a-57af-b124-47eb278a0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 